    "summary": "Hard To Handle roars, Mr. Charlie sparkles, tight jams all night",
    "blurb": "The August 6, 1971 Hollywood Palladium show delivers a blistering Hard To Handle and a standout Mr. Charlie, with the band riding high energy from start to finish. Highlights include a manic Cumberland Blues, a soaring The Other One sequence and a crisp, tight second‑set flow.",
    "review": "The Hollywood Palladium night of August 6, 1971 finds the Dead firing on all cylinders. Jerry’s solo on Hard To Handle is nothing short of explosive – fans report he even dropped to his knees at the climax – while Phil’s bass cuts through the matrix mix with manic clarity, driving each jam forward. Bob Weir adds vocal grit on Casey Jones and locks in a solid rhythm groove that fuels the set’s momentum. Pigpen’s organ work, though not front‑and‑center on every tape, shines on the audience captures that include his soulful fills. The second set is a masterclass in seamless segues: St. Stephen bursts into Truckin', which morphs into an extended The Other One/Me And My Uncle/The Other One sandwich, punctuated by a tight Deal and a jubilant Sugar Magnolia that leads into a heartfelt Morning Dew and Turn On Your Lovelight. Across the board the band is inspired, the crowd responsive, and the matrix/soundboard recordings capture every nuance with crystal‑clear detail, making this one of ’71’s most compelling documents.",
